{"name":"Butterfly Wing","hex":"#f8cfb4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"butterfly","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flying insect of the order Lepidoptera, distinguished from moths by their diurnal activity and generally brighter colouring. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}\n\n The imago (final form) of such an insect, consisting usually of a long body with large flat wings, as opposed to earlier instars such as caterpillar or pupa.","The imago (final form) of such an insect, consisting usually of a long body with large flat wings, as opposed to earlier instars such as caterpillar or pupa."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cut (food) almost entirely in half and spread the halves apart, in a shape suggesting the wings of a butterfly.","To cut strips of surgical tape or plasters into thin strips, and place across (a gaping wound) to close it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/butterfly"},{"term":"wing","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An appendage of an animal's (bird, bat, insect) body that enables it to fly.","A fin at the side of a ray or similar fish."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To injure slightly (as with a gunshot), especially in the wing or arm.","To fly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wing"}],"entries":[{"title":"Butterfly","blocks":[{"text":"Butterflies are winged insects from the lepidopteran superfamily Papilionoidea, characterised by large, often brightly coloured wings that often fold together when at rest, and a conspicuous, fluttering flight. The oldest butterfly fossils have been dated to the Paleocene, about 56 million years ago, though molecular evidence suggests that they likely originated in the Cretaceous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Fesoj_-_Papilio_machaon_%28by%29.jpg/330px-Fesoj_-_Papilio_machaon_%28by%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Butterfly","wikidata":"Q11946202"},{"title":"Wing","blocks":[{"text":"A wing is a structure which produces both lift and drag while moving through air. Wings are defined by two shape characteristics, an airfoil section and a planform. Wing efficiency is expressed as lift-to-drag ratio, which compares the benefit of lift with the air resistance of a given wing shape, as it flies. Aerodynamics includes the study of wing performance in air."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Wing.two.arp.600pix.jpg/330px-Wing.two.arp.600pix.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wing","wikidata":"Q161358"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg/500px-Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg/1280px-Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg/500px-Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Possibly the original butter-fly.[7] A male brimstone (Gonepteryx rhamni) in flight.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACommon_brimstone_butterfly_(Gonepteryx_rhamni)_male_in_flight.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Prodryas.png/500px-Prodryas.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm